// Tour - Code for one target.
//
// A line that starts with `#` is a directive. `#target` goes at the top of a
// file and says which targets the file is for. In any other target the file
// simply does not exist: its declarations are not even looked at. The words
// are architectures (`x64`), systems (`linux`, `windows`) or profiles (`debug`,
// `release`, `fast`), joined with `&`, `|`, `!` and parentheses:
// `#target x64 & linux`, or `#target x64 & !windows`.
//
// `#target` only filters. The files of a folder are one module, so linux.wsp
// and windows.wsp both declare `name` and `separator`, and in each build only
// the file for that target exists: this file just calls them, with no import
// and no condition.
//
// The same expressions work after `func`, `struct`, `enum`, `type`, `const`,
// `native` and `let` or `var` (for a global), for one declaration instead of a
// whole file: a version with a
// selector replaces the plain one of the same name when it matches, so the
// plain one is the default and each target can override it. Every version has
// to be the same kind of declaration, with the same signature and visibility.
// A name that exists only for other targets gets an error that says so.

import "std/fmt";

func lineEnd() string => "\n";

func<windows> lineEnd() string => "\r\n";

public func describe() string {
  return name();
}

public func demo() {
  fmt.println("this build is for {}, paths are separated with \"{}\", and a line ends with {} byte(s)", name(), separator(), len(lineEnd()));
}
